; Under the hood — what the optimizer does, and where it stops
; A guided read of the Cranelift IR the compiler actually emits. Each step
; defines a tiny function and shows its /clif — the real IR, no filtering.
; Two acts: the wins the optimizer lands today, then the frontier it stops at.

; Everything here is free-standing on primitives — bare ops, no prelude sugar —
; so the IR isolates exactly the optimization on display.

(import [primitives [*]])


; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; ACT 1 — THE WINS
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; 1. Scalars in a loop live in registers, not memory.
; Cranelisp has no loop form — iteration IS tail-self-recursion. A tail call
; lowers to a jump, and the loop-carried values ride the block parameters:

(defn sumto [:Int n :Int acc] (if (eq-i64 n 0) acc (sumto (sub-i64 n 1) (add-i64 acc n))))
/clif sumto

; Read the IR: `block1(v2, v3)` are the loop header's parameters — n and acc,
; in registers. The recursive tail is `jump block1(v9, v10)` — a back-edge, not
; a call, not a stack frame. No allocation anywhere. This is the ideal.


; 2. A single-field wrapper vanishes into a bare word.
; A one-constructor, one-scalar-field ADT carries no more information than its
; field, so the compiler represents it AS its field — no heap box, no header:

(deftype Cell (MkCell [:Int v]))
(defn unwrap [:Int n] (let [c (MkCell n)] (match c [(MkCell v) v])))
/clif unwrap

; Read the IR: `(MkCell n)` emits NOTHING. The argument v1 flows straight to
; `block2(v1)` and returns. No alloc, no store, no load — the value is a
; register the whole way through. Construction and match are free. Hold onto
; this: it is exactly single-field that gets this treatment (see Act 2).


; 3. Mutation happens in place when the value is unique.
; A vec write does not always copy. The compiler emits BOTH paths and picks at
; runtime on the refcount: rc==1 (unique) mutates in place; shared copies first
; (copy-on-write). A freshly built vec is unique, so this takes the fast arm:

(defn set-first [:Int n] (let [v (vec 1 2 3)] (vec-set v 0 n)))
/clif set-first

; Read the IR: `load v6+8` fetches the refcount, `icmp eq ..., 1` tests
; uniqueness, and `brif block2, block3` forks. block2 stores in place;
; block3 (`call fn1(...)`) is the copy-on-write fallback. Unique writes never
; copy.


; 4. Borrowing a value costs no reference-count traffic.
; A parameter the callee only reads is passed as a borrow — the caller keeps
; ownership, so there is no increment on entry and no decrement on exit:

(deftype Box (MkBox [:Int a :Int b]))
(defn get-h [:Box x] (match x [(MkBox a b) b]))
/clif get-h

; Read the IR: get-h reads the fields (`load x+24`, `load x+32`) and returns.
; There is not a single atomic_rmw in the function — the inc/dec pair a naive
; refcounting scheme would emit is elided entirely. Reads are free.


; 5. A small fixed record that never escapes goes on the stack, not the heap.
; When a value materialises (two constructors force a real object), is all
; scalar, and does not escape its frame, the backend puts it in a stack slot
; instead of calling the allocator:

(deftype Rect (Portrait [:Int portrait-w :Int portrait-h]) (Landscape [:Int landscape-w :Int landscape-h]))
(defn area [:Int a :Int b :Int flip] (let [r (if (eq-i64 flip 0) (Portrait a b) (Landscape a b))] (match r [(Portrait w h) (mul-i64 w h) (Landscape w h) (mul-i64 w h)])))
/clif area

; Read the IR: `ss0`/`ss1 = explicit_slot 40` are stack slots (one per arm),
; addressed by `stack_addr`. The header stores an immortal refcount
; `0x4000_0000_0000_0000` — so the residual `atomic_rmw sub` at the end can
; never reach zero and the free path is dead code. No allocator call at all.
; But note WHERE the fields live: `store ...v+24`, `load ...v+24` — stack
; MEMORY. That detail is the whole story of Act 2.


; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; ACT 2 — THE FRONTIER
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; 6. A multi-field record is never scalar-replaced into registers.
; pair-sum builds a two-field record whose constructor is statically known,
; that never escapes, in a straight-line (non-recursive) function — the BEST
; possible case. Compare it to `unwrap` from Act 1:

(deftype Pt (MkPt [:Int x :Int y]))
(defn pair-sum [:Int n] (let [p (MkPt n (add-i64 n 1))] (match p [(MkPt x y) (add-i64 x y)])))
/clif pair-sum

; Read the IR: even here the record lands in a stack slot (`explicit_slot 40`,
; `stack_addr`), and the two fields are written and read back through MEMORY
; (`store v+24`, `store v+32`, then `load v+24`, `load v+32`). The single-field
; Cell became a register; this two-field Pt does not. There is no multi-field
; SSA-decomposition (SROA) — value-flattening is single-field-only today. A
; stack slot is memory, not registers.


; 7. Put that record in a loop and it goes to the heap.
; The stack path is declined for ANY self-recursive function (a slot reused
; each iteration would clobber the live loop-carried value). Since every loop
; in Cranelisp is a self-recursive function, no loop body can stack-allocate:

(defn sum-areas [:Int k :Int acc] (if (eq-i64 k 0) acc (let [r (if (eq-i64 (sub-i64 k (mul-i64 (div-i64 k 2) 2)) 0) (Portrait k k) (Landscape k k))] (sum-areas (sub-i64 k 1) (add-i64 acc (match r [(Portrait w h) (mul-i64 w h) (Landscape w h) (mul-i64 w h)]))))))
/clif sum-areas

; Read the IR: the SAME Rect that stack-allocated in `area` now heap-allocates
; — `iconst.i64 24` (header + payload) into an allocator `call`, with a real
; `atomic_rmw sub` decrement and free path per iteration. The escape is not the
; problem; the self-call is. This gates out the entire iterative hot core.


; 8. A record with a heap field stays on the heap regardless.
; The stack path requires an all-scalar payload. One String field (itself a
; heap value) disqualifies the whole aggregate:

(deftype Tagged (TA [:Int ta-n :String ta-s]) (TB [:Int tb-n :String tb-s]))
(defn label [:Int n :String s :Int flip] (let [t (if (eq-i64 flip 0) (TA n s) (TB n s))] (match t [(TA k u) k (TB k u) k])))
/clif label

; Read the IR: heap `call` allocation, and an `atomic_rmw add` — the refcount
; increment keeping the String field alive inside the box. Nothing here can be
; a register or a stack slot.


; 9. A Vec local is heap-allocated — its size is dynamic.
; Even read-only and non-escaping, a Vec is a dynamically-sized buffer, so it
; cannot live in a fixed stack slot:

(defn vsum [:Int n] (let [v (vec n n n)] (vec-get v 0)))
/clif vsum

; Read the IR: an allocator `call` for the backing buffer, fields stored
; through the returned pointer, and an `atomic_rmw sub` + free on the way out.


; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; WHAT THE IR SAYS
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; The optimizer is strong on the scalar/unique/borrow axes: loop scalars are
; registers, single-field wrappers flatten to words, unique writes mutate in
; place, borrows carry no refcount traffic, and small escape-free scalar
; records dodge the allocator via a stack slot.
;
; The frontier is register-residency for AGGREGATES. A multi-field record never
; decomposes into SSA registers — the best case is a stack SLOT, which is
; memory; and that path is itself declined inside any loop (self-recursion) and
; for any heap-field or dynamically-sized value. The missing piece is
; multi-field SSA-decomposition (SROA / value-flattening beyond one word). That
; is the precondition for a future `--release` tier — LLVM's mem2reg/SROA
; promote such locals into registers, never onto the heap — to close the gap.
; It is the identified next direction, not a defect: the IR here is correct and
; conservative at every step.
;
; Every claim above is verified against the emitted IR in
; optimization-clif-verification.md (this directory).
